92186045417] [17592186149410 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ed2ef34e-9422-58a1-9455-6b2d57dcd007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149411 #edb/ref 1080 "\\div" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6828380-01ac-5a3c-855f-7a4e71d4487a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149412 #edb/ref 1080 "\\cdot" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7afaee0-d8a0-54dd-a041-2dc7bf61e8ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149413 #edb/ref 1080 "mod" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149413 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149413 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "310dd514-af1c-5159-a13d-b3cc2654b52b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151837 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151837 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $26 \\textrm{mod} 3$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c165bfd1-f6cc-5849-b519-f3763e481cbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151837 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < 3\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n26 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r$, we need to write $26$ as an integer multiple of $3$, plus the residue $r$.\n\nWe note that $26 \\equiv 2 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$ because\n\n$$\n26 = 8\\cdot 3 + {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property. Remember that any integer multiple of $3$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $3$.\n\n$$\n26 \\mid ≡26 - 0\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡26 - 8 \\cdot 3\\end{vmatrix}(mod3); ≡26 - 24\\begin{vmatrix}(mod3) \\\\ ≡2\\end{vmatrix}(mod3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151838 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151838 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $-13 \\textrm{mod} 4$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151838 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f664a47-49c5-5183-bdcc-42ba86553a67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151838 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is the integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < 4\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n-13 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r$, we need to write $-13$ as an integer multiple of $4$, plus the residue $r$.\n\nWe note that $-13 \\equiv 3 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-13 = (-4)\\cdot 4 + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property. Remember that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4$.\n\n$$\n-13 \\mid ≡ - 13 + 0\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡ - 13 + 4 \\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}(mod4); ≡ - 13 + 16\\begin{vmatrix}(mod4) \\\\ ≡3\\end{vmatrix}(mod4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151857 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151857 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "895dcb35-e68c-5b67-8f8a-8bc895bbde8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151857 #edb/ref 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nb=24a+15\n$$\n\nNote that $b$ is positive. Now, let $d_3$, $d_2$, and $d_1$ be the last three digits of the number $b{:}$\n\n$$\nb = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= ⋯ + 100d_{3} + 10d_{2} + d_{1} \\\\\n&= 10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\nb \\mid ≡10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 \\cdot (⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡0 + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nNotice that $d_1$ is the last digit of $b$.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nb = 24a+15\n$$\n\nand\n$24\\equiv 4, \\qquad 15\\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,10)$\nwe have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}b & ≡d_{1} & (mod10) \\\\ 24a + 15 & ≡d_{1} & (mod10) \\\\ 4a + 5 & ≡d_{1} & (mod10)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(4a+5) \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152055 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152055 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152055 #edb/ref 1039 "Modular Residues of Positive Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152055 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152055 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1ffdfb29-a3d2-5a7a-868c-21eab8965922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152056 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152056 #edb/ref 1039 "Modular Residues of Negative Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152056 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152056 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e70cef00-bf25-5423-85ac-2d3a44c68f35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152057 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152057 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152057 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Last Few Digits of a Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152057 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152057 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9a090bd2-cdb1-5680-81bc-3cb36de126c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1045 "Modular Residues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186152888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152370 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6bd8a7d5-2678-5b54-9b66-62a2a997915c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152476 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $a$.$\\div$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94462753-c983-5b18-8533-8c7f9bc5c0ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152888 #edb/ref 1061 127416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f710312-c2db-520c-8c32-4e476000c4b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152889 #edb/ref 1061 127417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a747838-602d-56a3-9a08-6fe0715111e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152890 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152890 #edb/ref 1061 127420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152890 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e1b37d9-7e44-5eb0-83e1-21869f3f0309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152890 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152891 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152891 #edb/ref 1061 127423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152891 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11d64553-4cfe-57ab-b748-7eff7b1ecdf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152891 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152892 #edb/ref 1061 127424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "529632e0-a417-5b9a-a825-90ea5c21dd4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1064 "The **residue** of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the *unique* integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\na \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThis integer $r$ is sometimes called the **least residue** of $a$ modulo $n$.\n\nFor example, consider the following statements:\n\n$$\n25\\begin{vmatrix}≡1 & (mod6) \\\\ 25 & ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); 25\\begin{vmatrix}≡13 & (mod6) \\\\ 25 & ≡19\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ⋮\n$$\n\nAll of these statements are true. However, we say that \"the residue of $25$ modulo $6$ is ${\\color{blue}{1}}$\", because\n\n$$\n0\\leq {\\color{blue}{1}} < 6\n$$\n\nWe write this as\n\n$$\n25 \\textrm{mod} 6 = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that there are no parentheses around the $\\textrm{mod}$. When there are no parentheses, it means that we're treating $\\textrm{mod}$ as though it were a type of function. This function takes our integer $25$ as its input and returns the remainder that we get when $25$ is divided by $6$.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n13mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n19mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll present two methods of computing residues. Make sure that you understand *both* methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1065 9014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "25e74a5e-486f-54d0-816c-972f89285fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153464 #edb/ref 1064 "Some interesting problems in number theory require finding the last few digits of a large integer. We can use properties of congruences to help us solve these types of problems.\n\nConsider a positive number $a$, where the digit $d_1$ is in the ones place, $d_2$ is in the tens place, $d_3$ is in the hundreds place, and so on.\n\n$$\na = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= ⋯ + 100d_{3} + 10d_{2} + d_{1} \\\\\n&= 10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\na \\mid ≡10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 \\cdot (⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡0 + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10$ returns the last digit of $a$.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n25\\,409\\,19{\\color{blue}3} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 10 = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nUsing similar reasoning,\n\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 100$ returns the last two digits of $a$, e.g.,\n$25\\,409\\,1{\\color{blue}93} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 100 = {\\color{blue}93}$.\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 1\\,000$ returns the last three digits of $a$, e.g.,\n$25\\,409\\,{\\color{blue}193} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 1\\,000 = {\\color{blue}193}$,\n\nand so on." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153464 #edb/ref 1065 9041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153464 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "399f1060-ce12-53e6-b522-98102a696b86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153464 #edb/ref 1067 "Residues Modulo Powers of Ten" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
